.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH SINH 3M
.SH NAME
sinh, cosh, tanh \- hyperbolic functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double sinh(double x);
.PP
.B double cosh(double x);
.PP
.B double tanh(double x);
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions compute the designated hyperbolic functions
for real arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
exp(3), sin(3), intro(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Sinh
and
.I cosh
return HUGE_VAL of appropriate sign
when the correct value would overflow, and set
.I errno
to ERANGE.
.B Note:
v7's page did not mention
.IR errno .
The value comes from
.IR exp (3),
which is where the gate is; see BESM-6 NOTES.
.PP
.I Tanh
is bounded by 1 and has no error return.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The thresholds were mantissa widths, and had to be rederived
For a large argument the exp(\-x) term stops contributing and all three routines
drop it.  v7 puts that point at
.BR 21 ,
which is where it dies against a
.I 56-bit
PDP-11 mantissa.  The mantissa here is 40 bits, so the term dies at
20*ln2 = 13.86: past |x| = 14, exp(\-2x) is already below 2**\-40, and exp(x) \-
exp(\-x) and exp(x) agree to the last bit.
.B All three routines switch at 14.
.SS The large branch is spelled exp(x \- ln2), not exp(x)/2
Identical arithmetic, and the difference is which routine raises the error.
.PP
.IR Exp (3)
gates its own argument at 43.6 = ln(DBL_MAX), so
.B exp(x)/2
would come back as a saturated HUGE_VAL/2 for
.I x
in (43.6, 44.3] \- where the true
.IR sinh ,
being half of exp(x), is still perfectly representable.  Writing it
.B exp(x \- ln2)
moves
.IR exp 's
gate out to x = 44.3 = ln(2*DBL_MAX), which is exactly where
.I sinh
itself overflows, and lets
.I exp
raise the ERANGE at the right place.
.PP
This matters more here than it would elsewhere, because on this machine an
overflow past 2**63 is an arithmetic fault that
.B kills the program,
not an infinity to return.  A gate one tenth too loose does not produce a wrong
number; it produces no number at all.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libm.a
.br
lib/libm/sinh.c, tanh.c
